Lagrange Holler
Ingredients: SOUTHERN ROCK + CELESTIAL, SPACE & ORBITAL + Steel Drums
Creative Catalyst: Born in a secluded Appalachian cloister where Trappist hermits wire their rapid-eye-movement sleep cycles into modular voltage systems to steer phantom satellites.

Lagrange Holler sounds like a dust-caked backwoods revival blasted out of the airlock into deep orbit. Grease-stained twin Les Paul slide guitars trade soaring, whiskey-drenched blues harmonies with crystalline Trinidadian steel pans, while modular synthesizer walls—driven by the neuro-voltages of sleeping monks—tear open cosmic voids beneath a relentless swamp-boogie shuffle.

Genre DNA
* Tempo & Pulse: 88–118 BPM; a heavy, syncopated Southern roadhouse swing colliding violently with weightless, polyrhythmic 7/8 orbital steel pan countermelodies.
* Harmonic Palette: Raw Mixolydian blues inflections, swampy open-E slide tunings, and sudden, dizzying shifts into shimmering Lydian celestial clusters and microtonal overtone series.
* Production Signatures: "Cryo-pan tracking" (steel drums struck with nitrogen-frozen mallets inside vacuum tanks for impossibly long sustain), "Somno-CV Modulation" (Eurorack control voltages routed directly from monastic sleep monitors to overmodulate tube amp bias), and rotary Leslie speakers mic'd inside 60-foot decommissioned missile silos.
* Dynamics: Expansive and elastic; tracks begin as solitary porch-side slide laments before detonating into colossal, supernova-level twin-guitar and steel-drum crescendos that collapse into zero-gravity synth ambience.

Scene & Origin Lore
In 1994, inside the St. Jude-in-the-Pines Cloister tucked into the Chattahoochee foothills, a renegade order of Benedictine-trained mystics began soldering Soviet surplus patch cords to EEG helmets. Believing that REM sleep is the uncorrupted frequency of the divine cosmos, the brothers routed their nocturnal neuro-currents into homemade modular synth rigs, eventually beaming the signals toward abandoned geosynchronous satellites. When rogue moonshiners and ex-Allman roadies set up a pirate receiver camp on the ridge with a stolen flatbed full of oil barrels and Trinidadian steel pans, the frequencies crossed. The resulting subculture—dubbed "The Orbiting Flock"—gathers in abandoned satellite tracking stations wearing denim overalls bleached with bleach-pen star charts, cowl-necked habits stitched from flame-retardant NASA flight suits, and rosaries beaded with burned-out 12AX7 vacuum tubes. Their central ritual, the Stellafast, involves forty-eight hours of non-stop slide playing and sleep-patching fueled by charred sorghum wine until the boundary between the swamp and deep space evaporates.

Typical Instruments
* Tuned Trinidadian Steel Drums: High-tenor and double-second steel pans treated with cosmic flange, stereo tape delays, and liquid-nitrogen mallet damping.
* 1950s Gibson Les Pauls & Resonator Guitars: Slung low, played with solid brass slides through dimed, spitting 100-watt tube heads.
* Monastic Sleep-CV Modular Rigs: Custom 5U and Eurorack synth systems that convert real-time human brainwaves into sweeping resonant low-pass filters and astral sub-bass.
* Rotary B3 Organs & Mellotrons: Providing celestial chorales, orbital radio whines, and swamp-gospel chordal bedrock.

